{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is edge AI deployment?

Edge AI deployment runs machine-learning inference where data is created: on a
phone, camera, sensor, vehicle, or nearby gateway. The critical request does not need
to cross the internet before the product can make a decision.

In plain language: a cloud model is only a starting point. The production system must
compile an appropriate model for each hardware tier, keep a trusted copy available
offline, and update millions of devices without turning one bad release into a
fleet-wide failure.

This case study uses a mobile-camera feature as the concrete scenario. It designs a
privacy-first inference path for **10M active devices** while accounting for IoT-class
hardware, battery limits, unreliable connectivity, and staged model delivery.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Start with the on-device promise

The product contract determines the architecture. A camera feature should remain
responsive and useful during an outage, and raw frames should not leave the device
merely because inference is easier in the cloud.

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Respond while the moment is current" eyebrow="Latency" icon="gauge" tone="blue" %}
Keep critical mobile inference below **50ms**. Network variance cannot consume a budget
that belongs to capture, preprocessing, model execution, and post-processing.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Keep sensitive input local" eyebrow="Privacy" icon="lock" tone="green" %}
Process raw pixels and sensor data on device. Export only explicitly approved,
privacy-reduced telemetry rather than defaulting to raw-data collection.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Continue through an outage" eyebrow="Availability" icon="shield" tone="violet" %}
The last-known-good model, preprocessing code, and product fallback must remain usable
without the control plane or an update connection.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Fit the actual hardware" eyebrow="Efficiency" icon="cpu" tone="amber" %}
Target less than **50MB** for a primary mobile model, less than **200MB** peak runtime
memory, and less than **5% battery drain per hour** for continuous workloads.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="10M" label="Active devices" detail="A release is a distributed-systems event" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="1B" label="Daily inferences" detail="About 100 decisions per active device" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="1,000+" label="Hardware profiles" detail="Operators and accelerators vary by tier" tone="amber" /%}
{% metric value="99.9%" label="Offline availability" detail="The local path owns the product promise" tone="green" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

{% callout variant="info" %}
### Core invariant

The currently active local model must remain complete, compatible, and callable until
a replacement has been downloaded, authenticated, verified, and proven healthy.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Place the model against real constraints

Model choice and execution location are one decision. A larger model can improve task
quality, but it also changes runtime memory, accelerator time, battery use, privacy,
and offline behavior. Use the lab to find configurations that satisfy the whole
contract rather than one benchmark.

{% interactive-block id="case-studies/edge-ai-deployment-placement-lab" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% accordion defaultOpen="architecture" %}

{% accordion-item id="architecture" title="1. Separate the inference path from the control plane" %}

## The local path serves users; the control plane manages change

The synchronous path should contain only the work needed to transform sensor input
into a product decision. Model optimization, rollout coordination, telemetry analysis,
and federated aggregation belong outside that latency budget.

{% system-flow title="On-device camera decision path" caption="Every stage needed for the immediate result remains on the device. Cloud connectivity can improve and update the system, but it is not required for one inference." %}
{% system-node title="Capture and buffer" eyebrow="Input" icon="camera" tone="cyan" %}
Bound the frame buffer, select the useful region, and discard data that the feature
does not need.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Preprocess locally" eyebrow="Features" icon="settings" tone="blue" %}
Resize, normalize, and transform the input exactly as expected by the compiled model.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Run the device model" eyebrow="Inference" icon="cpu" tone="violet" %}
Use the compatible CPU, GPU, NPU, or DSP execution provider chosen for this hardware
tier.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Apply the product decision" eyebrow="Output" icon="sparkles" tone="green" %}
Check confidence, apply business rules, render the feature, and queue optional
privacy-safe signals for later synchronization.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

The asynchronous control plane has different responsibilities:

- **Model build plane**
  - Quantizes, prunes, distills, converts, and signs hardware-specific artifacts.
  - Benchmarks latency, memory, power, supported operators, and task quality per tier.
- **Fleet control plane**
  - Maps device capability to compatible artifacts and rollout cohorts.
  - Delivers deltas, records activation health, pauses promotion, and coordinates rollback.
- **Learning and observability plane**
  - Aggregates privacy-reduced crash, latency, thermal, and quality signals.
  - Detects drift by model version and hardware profile without placing telemetry in the critical path.

{% callout variant="tip" %}
Cloud inference may still be useful for an optional high-quality enhancement. It must
not silently become the fallback for a feature whose contract promises offline use or
local privacy.
{% /callout %}
{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="scale" title="2. Turn fleet size into delivery and telemetry budgets" %}

## A small per-device cost becomes a large fleet event

The useful calculation is not only model size. Multiply every artifact, retry, and
telemetry record by the eligible cohort, then budget for devices that reconnect in
bursts after hours or days offline.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="8TB" label="One 0.8MB delta" detail="Across 10M eligible devices before retries" tone="green" /%}
{% metric value="340TB" label="One 34MB full model" detail="A full-fleet replacement before CDN overhead" tone="rose" /%}
{% metric value="10GB/day" label="Minimal telemetry" detail="At 1KB per active device per day" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="500K" label="Retries at 5%" detail="Why jitter and cohort pacing matter" tone="amber" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

Plan capacity around these behaviors:

- **Reconnect bursts:** devices should add jitter and use resumable downloads rather than synchronizing immediately.
- **Regional concentration:** CDN and object-storage egress must tolerate cohorts that are not geographically uniform.
- **Compatibility fan-out:** one logical model version may compile into many artifacts for different operators and chipsets.
- **Telemetry sampling:** aggregate counters and sketches usually answer fleet-health questions more cheaply than raw event streams.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
A 1% canary limits product blast radius, but it does not automatically limit delivery
load. Targeting errors, retry loops, or a missing compatibility filter can still send
an artifact to the wrong devices repeatedly.
{% /callout %}
{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="optimization" title="3. Optimize a model without hiding quality loss" %}

## Compression is a release decision, not a file-conversion step

Optimization succeeds only when the compiled artifact retains the product behavior
that matters. Validate the full preprocessing and post-processing pipeline, not just a
standalone model benchmark.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Quantize precision" eyebrow="Memory and compute" icon="gauge" tone="blue" %}
INT8 commonly reduces weight storage about fourfold compared with FP32 and can unlock
specialized accelerator kernels. INT4 can reduce further, but operator support and
task-specific quality require direct measurement.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Remove unnecessary work" eyebrow="Architecture" icon="scissors" tone="violet" %}
Structured pruning, smaller inputs, efficient operators, and hardware-aware neural
architecture search reduce work that the runtime can actually skip.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Distill product behavior" eyebrow="Quality" icon="sparkles" tone="green" %}
Train a smaller student from a stronger teacher, then compare the student on slices
that represent low light, motion, device sensors, and other real edge conditions.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

For every hardware tier, make the release report answer:

1. Does the artifact load with the available execution provider and supported operators?
2. What are cold-start and warm p50/p95 latency under realistic thermal conditions?
3. What are peak memory and sustained battery costs, not only average CPU use?
4. Which quality slices regress relative to the cloud teacher and current production model?
5. What simpler model or non-ML behavior takes over when the primary model cannot run?
{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="offline" title="4. Design offline behavior as a complete state machine" %}

## Offline-first means more than caching a model file

The local runtime needs enough state and policy to continue making safe decisions
when it cannot authenticate with, report to, or download from the control plane.

- **Input state:** bound camera and sensor buffers so an outage cannot create unbounded local storage.
- **Inference state:** keep the active artifact, tokenizer or feature transforms, labels, and confidence policy versioned together.
- **Output state:** distinguish immediate product output from optional records waiting to synchronize.
- **Update state:** download into an inactive slot, resume partial transfers, verify the complete artifact, and switch atomically.
- **Recovery state:** retain a last-known-good artifact and a non-ML fallback when storage or hardware health is degraded.

When connectivity returns, synchronization should be opportunistic and bounded:

1. Refresh security and rollout policy before requesting a model.
2. Resume the smallest eligible delta with randomized backoff.
3. Upload only approved aggregate signals from a priority queue.
4. Resolve conflicts with product-specific rules rather than assuming last-write-wins is always correct.
5. Delete expired local data according to a documented retention policy.
{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="privacy" title="5. Learn from the fleet without collecting raw input by default" %}

## Privacy controls shape both observability and model improvement

On-device inference reduces data movement, but it does not make the system private by
itself. Logs, cached inputs, model updates, identifiers, and diagnostic uploads can
still reveal sensitive behavior.

- **Minimize collection:** emit versioned counters for latency, crashes, thermal throttling, confidence bands, and update state.
- **Protect transport and artifacts:** sign model manifests, verify hashes, encrypt delivery, and attest supported devices where the threat model requires it.
- **Bound retention:** encrypt sensitive local state and delete it when the feature no longer needs it.
- **Aggregate before analysis:** use sampling, thresholded reporting, or differential privacy when individual device signals are unnecessary.
- **Treat federated learning as a system:** select eligible devices, cap local work, tolerate dropouts, use secure aggregation, and evaluate the resulting global model independently.

Federated learning can avoid centralizing raw training examples, but gradients and
updates are not automatically anonymous. Privacy budgets, secure aggregation, device
selection, poisoning defenses, and user controls remain explicit design decisions.
{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="tradeoffs" title="6. Record the decisions and their escape hatches" %}

## Choose from the product contract, then name when the choice breaks

- **Tiered models instead of one universal artifact**
  - Gains broader hardware coverage and better use of accelerators.
  - Costs more build, validation, storage, and rollout complexity.
  - Revisit when most active devices converge on a compatible hardware baseline.
- **On-device primary inference with optional cloud enhancement**
  - Protects latency, privacy, and offline behavior for the critical feature.
  - Limits primary-model size and makes hardware-specific optimization mandatory.
  - Route to cloud only when the user benefit is optional and the data policy allows it.
- **Delta delivery with a retained rollback slot**
  - Reduces bandwidth and keeps a complete current artifact available during failure.
  - Uses extra storage and requires atomic activation logic.
  - Fall back to a full signed artifact when the delta base does not match exactly.
- **Staged rollout instead of fleet-wide activation**
  - Bounds compatibility, crash, latency, and quality regressions to a cohort.
  - Slows promotion and requires representative canaries plus trustworthy health gates.
  - Pause automatically when any guardrail crosses its predefined threshold.
- **Privacy-reduced telemetry instead of raw event collection**
  - Lowers data exposure and fleet bandwidth.
  - Makes debugging rare device-specific failures harder.
  - Add consented, time-bounded diagnostics rather than weakening the default for everyone.
{% /accordion-item %}

{% /accordion %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Pressure-test offline updates and rollback

The update mechanism is part of availability. A device that worked before an update
should not lose its only complete model because a download stopped, a signature failed,
or the candidate passed technical checks but regressed product quality.

{% interactive-block id="case-studies/edge-ai-deployment-resilience-lab" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Close the edge deployment loop

Optimization, local validation, fleet control, and privacy-safe learning form one
release loop. Each stage produces evidence required by the next stage.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Build per hardware tier" label="Compile" %}

Quantize, prune, convert, sign, and package compatible model-plus-runtime bundles.

{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Prove the local contract" label="Validate" %}

Test cold and warm latency, memory, battery, task quality, offline state, and fallback behavior on representative devices.

{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Promote through cohorts" label="Control" %}

Start with employees or labs, then 1%, 10%, and broader cohorts while automatic health gates protect promotion.

{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Learn and recover" label="Operate" %}

Aggregate approved telemetry by version and hardware tier, detect drift, roll back quickly, and feed evidence into the next release.

{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

{% callout variant="tip" %}
### Minimum release evidence

- Compatibility manifest for every targeted device profile
- Signed artifact and reproducible model version
- Slice-level quality comparison against the current production model
- Offline, interrupted-download, storage-pressure, and rollback tests
- Cohort health gates for crashes, latency, thermal cost, and product quality
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

